CHUNK({"chunk":"A","module":"m1","topics":[{"id":"m1-evs-intro","title":"Environmental studies: definition, scope and importance","short":"What EVS is","blurb":"Environmental science vs environmental studies vs environmental engineering, the ten areas EVS covers, why it matters, and why it needs many subjects at once.","source":"Lecture W1S1, pages 1–5","tabs":[{"title":"Three definitions","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td><b>Environmental science</b></td><td>Studies human–natural system interactions to understand and solve environmental problems.</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Environmental studies</b></td><td>Interdisciplinary; stresses <b>ethical, social, political, economic</b> sides; seeks sustainable solutions.</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Environmental engineering</b></td><td>Engineering + science to <b>preserve and enhance</b> quality: waste management, pollution prevention, water/air purification, sustainable development.</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Encyclopedia Britannica</b>: interdisciplinary, drawing on ecology, geology, meteorology, biology, chemistry, engineering, physics.</li>\n            <li><b>Spellman &amp; Stoudt</b>: study of <b>how the Earth works</b> and how we affect its life support systems.</li>\n            <li>Studies also links <b>history, politics, public policy, law</b> to the issues.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"note trap\"><p>Studies = people, ethics, policy. Science = how nature works. Engineering = technology.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Scope","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>What is studied</th><th>Area</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Living things and surroundings</td><td><b>Ecology and ecosystem studies</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Weather, air pollution, climate change</td><td><b>Atmospheric science</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Geologic processes, soil, landforms, resources</td><td><b>Geoscience</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Freshwater management, quality, conservation</td><td><b>Hydrology and water resources</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Air, water, soil pollution: sources, impacts, solutions</td><td><b>Pollution control</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Maintaining species and habitats</td><td><b>Biodiversity and conservation</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Chemical processes and impacts</td><td><b>Environmental chemistry</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Laws, rules, moral dilemmas</td><td><b>Environmental policy and management</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Meet current needs without harming future generations</td><td><b>Sustainable development</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Effects of population growth and urban growth</td><td><b>Human population and urbanization</b></td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n        "},{"title":"Importance","html":"\n          <ol>\n            <li>Understand global issues: <b>pollution, deforestation, climate change, biodiversity loss</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>Sustainable use</b> of resources.</li>\n            <li>Conserve <b>soil, water, air, energy</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>Identify pollution sources</b>; build tech and policy to cut it.</li>\n            <li>Cut hazards to <b>human health</b>.</li>\n            <li>Encourage <b>responsible behaviour</b> and awareness.</li>\n            <li><b>Predict and manage disasters</b> (floods, droughts, earthquakes).</li>\n          </ol>\n        "},{"title":"Multidisciplinary approach","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\"><b>Multidisciplinary approach</b>: integrating knowledge and methods from many disciplines to understand and solve environmental problems.</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li>Issues are <b>complex and interconnected</b>; one subject can't solve them.</li>\n            <li>EVS covers <b>every issue that affects an organism</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>Applied science</b>: seeks practical ways to live sustainably on Earth's <b>finite resources</b>.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Discipline</th><th>Contributes</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td><b>Biology</b></td><td>Ecosystems, biodiversity, species interactions</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Chemistry</b></td><td>Pollution, chemical cycles (C, N), toxicity</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Physics</b></td><td>Energy flow, climate modelling, solar radiation</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Geology</b></td><td>Soil, minerals, disasters, landforms</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Geography</b></td><td>Mapping, spatial relations, planning</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Economics</b></td><td>Cost-benefit, resource management, sustainable development</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Political science</b></td><td>Laws, treaties, policy-making</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Sociology</b></td><td>Behaviour, cultural impact, awareness</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Engineering</b></td><td>Pollution control tech, renewable energy</td></tr>\n            <tr><td><b>Ethics / philosophy</b></td><td>Moral responsibility, environmental justice</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n        "},{"title":"Why many subjects","html":"\n          <ol>\n            <li><b>Holistic understanding</b>: issues interlink (climate → farming, health, water).</li>\n            <li><b>Effective problem solving</b>: water scarcity needs <b>hydrology</b> (science), <b>policy</b> (law), <b>infrastructure</b> (engineering).</li>\n            <li><b>Sustainable development</b>: balance ecology, economy, social equity.</li>\n            <li><b>Informed decision-making</b> by governments, industry, individuals.</li>\n            <li><b>Policy formulation and implementation</b>: needs science, society, law, economics.</li>\n            <li><b>Adaptability</b>: new threats like <b>microplastics</b>, pandemics from habitat loss.</li>\n          </ol>\n          <div class=\"note exam\"><p>Long answer: write the six headings above, and use water scarcity as the example under problem solving.</p></div>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Field that stresses the ethical, social, political and economic sides of environmental problems?","back":"<b>Environmental studies</b>."},{"front":"Spellman &amp; Stoudt define environmental science as…","back":"The interdisciplinary study of <b>how the Earth works</b> and how we are affecting the Earth's life support systems."},{"front":"Field that uses engineering and scientific principles to preserve and enhance environmental quality?","back":"<b>Environmental engineering</b>."}],"cardCount":10,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m1-env-issues","title":"Major environmental issues","short":"Environmental issues","blurb":"Eight issues with their causes, effects and Indian examples, the global picture with its numbers, India's situation, and loss of biodiversity.","source":"Lecture W1S1, pages 5–11","tabs":[{"title":"Climate change","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Write each issue as <b>cause → effects → example</b>.</p>\n          <h3>1. Climate change</h3>\n          <p><b>Cause:</b> <b>greenhouse gases</b> (CO<sub>2</sub>, CH<sub>4</sub>, N<sub>2</sub>O) from <b>fossil fuels, deforestation, industry</b> trap heat.</p>\n          <p><b>Effects:</b></p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Rising global temperatures</b> (global warming).</li>\n            <li><b>Melting ice caps, rising sea levels</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>Severe weather</b>: droughts, floods, hurricanes.</li>\n            <li>Harms <b>biodiversity and farming</b>.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><p>IPCC: about <b>1.1 °C</b> rise since pre-industrial times.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Deforestation","html":"\n          <h3>2. Deforestation</h3>\n          <p><b>Cause:</b> <b>logging, agriculture, urban expansion, mining</b>.</p>\n          <p><b>Effects:</b></p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Loss of biodiversity and habitats</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>Disrupted water cycle</b>: less moisture, changed rainfall.</li>\n            <li><b>More CO<sub>2</sub></b>: <b>carbon sinks</b> lost, stored carbon released.</li>\n            <li><b>Soil erosion, desertification</b>.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><p><b>Amazon rainforest</b> deforestation adds significantly to global carbon emissions.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Waste, air and water","html":"\n          <h3>3. Waste management</h3>\n          <p><b>Types:</b> municipal solid, plastic, organic, landfill overflow.</p>\n          <p><b>Problems:</b></p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Overflowing landfills</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>Methane</b> from decomposing organics.</li>\n            <li><b>Poor segregation and recycling</b>.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <p><b>Remedies:</b> <b>3Rs</b> (Reduce-Reuse-Recycle), <b>composting, waste-to-energy</b>.</p>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><p><b>CPCB (2021)</b>: India makes over <b>150,000 tonnes</b> MSW/day.</p></div>\n          <h3>4. Industrial and vehicular pollution</h3>\n          <p><b>Causes:</b> industry: <b>emissions, effluents, PM2.5, PM10</b>; vehicles: <b>CO, NOx, hydrocarbons</b>.</p>\n          <p><b>Effects:</b> <b>respiratory disease, smog, acid rain</b>; pollutes <b>air, water, soil</b>.</p>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><p><b>Delhi</b>: among the world's most polluted cities.</p></div>\n          <h3>5. Water pollution</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Causes:</b> <b>agricultural runoff</b> (pesticides, <b>nitrates, phosphates</b>); <b>industrial effluents and sewage</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>Effects:</b> <b>eutrophication</b> (algal growth); <b>contaminated drinking water</b>; <b>health</b>: cancer, reproductive issues, endocrine disruption.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><p><b>Punjab groundwater</b>: <b>Endosulfan, DDT</b> residues.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Hazardous, crackers, e-waste","html":"\n          <h3>6. Hazardous waste</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Tannery sludge:</b> <b>chromium</b> sludge dumped → <b>heavy metals</b> in soil and groundwater; harms crops and health.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><p><b>Ranipet (Tamil Nadu)</b>: chromium in groundwater.</p></div>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Biomedical waste:</b> from <b>hospitals, clinics, labs</b>; risks <b>infections (HIV, hepatitis)</b>, toxic and radioactive material; managed by <b>segregation, incineration, autoclaving</b>.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><p><b>COVID-19</b>: surge strained disposal.</p></div>\n          <h3>7. Cracking down on crackers</h3>\n          <p>Smoke, PM and toxic gases (<b>SO<sub>2</sub>, NO, Pb</b>):</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Air pollution spikes</b> (Diwali).</li>\n            <li><b>Respiratory distress</b> in vulnerable people.</li>\n            <li><b>Noise</b> harms animals, elderly.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <p><b>Response:</b> <b>Supreme Court</b> bans/limits; <b>green crackers</b>.</p>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><p>Delhi AQI hits <b>\"Severe\"</b> after Diwali.</p></div>\n          <h3>8. E-waste</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Source:</b> discarded mobiles, computers, TVs.</li>\n            <li><b>Toxic metals</b> (lead, mercury, cadmium) leach into soil and water.</li>\n            <li><b>Informal recycling</b> endangers workers.</li>\n            <li><b>Solutions:</b> <b>formal recycling</b>, <b>EPR</b> (producer responsible after disposal).</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><p>India: <b>third-largest</b> generator, over <b>1 million tonnes/year</b> (CPCB, 2020).</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Global scenario","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Key fact</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Climate change</td><td>Fossil fuel GHGs; glacier melt, sea rise, extreme weather. <b>UNFCCC</b>, <b>Paris Agreement (2015)</b>: <b>below 2 °C</b>; IPCC urges action.</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Biodiversity loss</td><td><b>1 million species</b> at risk (deforestation, pollution, overfishing, habitat loss)</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Plastic, marine</td><td><b>8 million tons</b>+ plastic into oceans/year; microplastics in marine life, <b>human blood</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Air pollution</td><td><b>99%</b> breathe unsafe air; Beijing, Cairo smog</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>E-waste, resources</td><td><b>53 million metric tons</b> (2020); overextraction, geopolitical tension</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n        "},{"title":"India and biodiversity loss","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Drivers: <b>population density, industrial growth, urban expansion</b>.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>India</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Air</td><td><b>14 of 20</b> most polluted cities; Delhi (vehicles, <b>crop burning</b>, industry)</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Water</td><td><b>70%</b> surface water polluted (<b>Ganga, Yamuna</b>); pesticides in groundwater</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Forests, biodiversity</td><td>Lost to mining, farming, infrastructure; <b>10%</b> flora/fauna at risk</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Waste</td><td><b>160,000 t</b>+/day, ~<b>60%</b> collected, &lt;<b>30%</b> processed; e-waste 1 Mt+/year</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Climate</td><td><b>7th</b> in climate risk: floods, droughts, cyclones</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Hazardous</td><td>Medical/industrial dumping, e.g. <b>Ranipet</b> chromium</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n          <div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>Daily waste: 150,000 t (CPCB 2021, waste issue) vs 160,000 t (India scenario). Quote each in context.</p></div>\n          <h3>Loss of biodiversity</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Definition:</b> decline or disappearance of plant, animal, microbe species.</li>\n            <li><b>Causes:</b> <b>habitat destruction, pollution, climate change, overexploitation, invasive species</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>Effects:</b> weaker ecosystems, broken food chains, less life support.</li>\n            <li>Problems interlink, so a <b>multidisciplinary approach</b> gives sustainable solutions.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"note exam\"><p>Long answer: each of the 8 issues as cause → effects → remedy → example; then global numbers, India numbers, biodiversity loss, multidisciplinary need.</p></div>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Three greenhouse gases named as the cause of climate change?","back":"<b>CO<sub>2</sub>, CH<sub>4</sub>, N<sub>2</sub>O</b>."},{"front":"Global temperature rise since pre-industrial times (IPCC)?","back":"About <b>1.1 °C</b>."},{"front":"Example for deforestation?","back":"<b>Amazon rainforest</b>, a big contributor to global carbon emissions."}],"cardCount":12,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m1-earth-spheres","title":"Earth as a life support system: atmosphere and hydrosphere","short":"Atmosphere & water","blurb":"The four spheres, what air is made of, the five layers of the atmosphere with their heights and temperatures, and the hydrosphere and water cycle.","source":"Lecture W1S3, pages 1–8","tabs":[{"title":"Four spheres","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\"><b>Sphere</b> = <b>to surround or encompass</b>. Earth supplies four needs:</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Sphere</th><th>Root word</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Air</td><td><b>Atmosphere</b></td><td>atmo → steam and vapour</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Water</td><td><b>Hydrosphere</b></td><td>hydro → water</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Food</td><td><b>Biosphere</b></td><td>bio → life</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Ground, nutrients</td><td><b>Lithosphere</b></td><td>litho → rocks and minerals</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n          <p>The <b>biosphere depends on the other three</b>; together they keep Earth liveable.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-four-spheres\"></figure>\n        "},{"title":"Air and its layers","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Atmosphere: <b>gaseous layer</b> (gases + water particles) around Earth.</p>\n          <p>Gives oxygen, <b>blocks harmful solar radiation</b>, regulates temperature, drives weather and water cycle.</p>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Nitrogen</span><b>78%</b> by volume</div>\n            <div><span>Oxygen</span><b>21%</b></div>\n            <div><span>Others</span><b>1%</b>: water vapour, CO<sub>2</sub>, methane (key for climate)</div>\n          </div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-air-composition\"></figure>\n          <p>Layers: <b>troposphere → stratosphere → mesosphere → thermosphere → exosphere</b>.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-atmosphere-layers-photo\"></figure>\n          <div class=\"mnemonic\"><p>\"<b>T</b>he <b>S</b>trong <b>M</b>an <b>T</b>hrows <b>E</b>ggs\".</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Troposphere, stratosphere","html":"\n          <div class=\"vs\">\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Troposphere (0–15 km)</h4>\n              <ul>\n                <li><b>Lowest</b>; all life, birds, airplanes.</li>\n                <li><b>Most of the mass</b>, almost all weather.</li>\n                <li>Temperature <b>falls with height</b> (ground heats it).</li>\n                <li>Height varies with latitude, weather.</li>\n                <li>Top: <b>tropopause</b>, a lid on weather.</li>\n              </ul></div>\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Stratosphere (15–50 km)</h4>\n              <ul>\n                <li>Holds the <b>ozone layer</b>.</li>\n                <li>Ozone absorbs most <b>UV radiation</b>.</li>\n                <li>Temperature <b>rises with height</b> (ozone absorbs energy).</li>\n                <li><b>Stable, dry</b>: high aircraft, <b>weather balloons</b>.</li>\n                <li>Top: <b>stratopause</b>.</li>\n              </ul></div>\n          </div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-stratosphere-profile\"></figure>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-ozone-uv\"></figure>\n        "},{"title":"Mesosphere and above","html":"\n          <div class=\"vs\">\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Mesosphere (50–85 km)</h4>\n              <ul>\n                <li><b>Coldest</b>, about <b>−90 °C</b>.</li>\n                <li><b>Noctilucent clouds</b> (ice, seen at twilight).</li>\n                <li>Very thin air, hard to study.</li>\n                <li><b>Meteors burn up</b>.</li>\n                <li>Top: <b>mesopause</b>.</li>\n              </ul></div>\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Thermosphere</h4>\n              <ul>\n                <li>Temperature <b>rises</b> (absorbs <b>UV, X-rays</b>); top ~<b>500–2,000 °C</b>.</li>\n                <li><b>Auroras</b>.</li>\n                <li>Satellites, <b>ISS</b> orbit here.</li>\n              </ul></div>\n            <div class=\"side\"><h4>Exosphere</h4>\n              <ul>\n                <li><b>Outermost</b> (\"final frontier\").</li>\n                <li>Air <b>leaks into space</b>; no clear top.</li>\n                <li>Extremely thin; satellites.</li>\n              </ul></div>\n          </div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-mesosphere-diagram\"></figure>\n        "},{"title":"Layers at a glance","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Altitude (km)</th><th>Temperature (°C)</th><th>Feature</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Troposphere</td><td><b>0–15</b></td><td>15 to −56</td><td>Weather</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Stratosphere</td><td><b>15–50</b></td><td>−56 to −2</td><td>Ozone layer</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Mesosphere</td><td><b>50–85</b></td><td>−2 to −92</td><td>Meteors burn</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Thermosphere</td><td><b>85–800</b></td><td>−92 to 1200</td><td>Auroras</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-atmosphere-poster\"></figure>\n          <div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>Sources clash (thermosphere 2,000 vs 1200 °C; poster troposphere 0–12 km). Use <b>0–15, 15–50, 50–85 km</b>.</p></div>\n        "},{"title":"Hydrosphere and water cycle","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Hydrosphere: <b>all water</b> on, under and above Earth: liquid, ice, vapour.</p>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Oceans</span><b>~97%</b></div>\n            <div><span>Lakes and rivers</span>~2%</div>\n            <div><span>Usable freshwater</span>only <b>1%</b></div>\n          </div>\n          <p>Sustains life, <b>distributes heat</b> (climate); includes glaciers, groundwater, atmospheric water.</p>\n          <div class=\"note heads-up\"><p>Write the notes' 2% for lakes and rivers, though most of it is really ice.</p></div>\n          <h3>Water (hydrologic) cycle</h3>\n          <ol class=\"steps\">\n            <li><b>Evaporation</b> Sun turns water to vapour.</li>\n            <li><b>Condensation</b> Vapour cools into clouds.</li>\n            <li><b>Precipitation</b> Rain, snow fall.</li>\n            <li><b>Runoff / infiltration</b> Into rivers or soil.</li>\n            <li><b>Collection</b> In oceans, lakes; repeats.</li>\n          </ol>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-water-cycle-spheres\"></figure>\n          <div class=\"note exam\"><p>Long answer: spheres + roots, atmosphere (78/21/1), layers with height, trend, feature, boundary, hydrosphere (97/2/1), water cycle.</p></div>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does \"sphere\" mean?","back":"To <b>surround or encompass</b>."},{"front":"Root meaning of \"atmo\"?","back":"<b>Steam and vapour</b>."},{"front":"Which sphere depends on the other three?","back":"The <b>biosphere</b>."}],"cardCount":12,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m1-geosphere","title":"Geosphere","short":"Geosphere","blurb":"Earth's interior layers, rocks, minerals, soil and landforms, the processes that shape the surface, why the geosphere matters, and how it interacts with the other spheres.","source":"Lecture W1S3, pages 8–12","tabs":[{"title":"What it is and its layers","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Geosphere: <b>solid Earth</b>, <b>inner core to crust</b>: rocks, minerals, soil, landforms.</p>\n          <p>Base for land life; drives <b>plate tectonics, mountain formation, erosion</b>.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Inner core</td><td><b>Solid</b>, <b>iron and nickel</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Outer core</td><td><b>Liquid</b>; makes <b>magnetic field</b></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Mantle</td><td><b>Semi-solid</b>; <b>convection currents</b> drive plates</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Crust</td><td>Outermost; <b>continental and oceanic plates</b></td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-earth-layers\"></figure>\n        "},{"title":"Rocks, minerals, soil, landforms","html":"\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Rocks</span>Natural solid aggregates of minerals/mineraloids: <b>igneous, sedimentary, metamorphic</b>.</div>\n            <div><span>Minerals</span>Natural <b>inorganic</b> solids, <b>definite chemical composition</b>, <b>crystalline structure</b>.</div>\n            <div><span>Soil</span>From <b>weathering of rocks</b> + <b>decay of organic matter</b>; minerals, organics, water, air.</div>\n            <div><span>Landforms</span>Mountains, valleys, plateaus, volcanoes, faults; from <b>tectonic processes</b> + erosion.</div>\n          </div>\n          <h3>Geological processes</h3>\n          <ol>\n            <li><b>Plate tectonics</b>: earthquakes, volcanoes, mountains.</li>\n            <li><b>Weathering and erosion</b>: wind, water, ice.</li>\n            <li><b>Sedimentation</b>: layers harden into rock.</li>\n          </ol>\n        "},{"title":"Importance and interactions","html":"\n          <h3>Importance</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Supports ecosystems</b>: soil, habitats.</li>\n            <li><b>Natural resources</b>: minerals, metals, fossil fuels.</li>\n            <li><b>Affects climate</b>: volcanic gas and ash.</li>\n            <li><b>Shapes geography</b>: terrain, settlement.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <h3>Interactions</h3>\n          <p>Links with atmosphere, hydrosphere, biosphere, <b>cryosphere</b>.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Sphere</th><th>Interaction</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Atmosphere</td><td>Volcanoes release <b>CO<sub>2</sub>, ash</b>; wind, rain weather rock</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Hydrosphere</td><td>Rivers <b>erode</b>, carry sediment; groundwater moves minerals</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Biosphere</td><td><b>Roots break rocks</b>, add organics; burrowers change soil</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n          <div class=\"note exam\"><p>Long answer: definition, 4 layers, rocks/minerals/soil/landforms, 3 processes, 4 importance points, 3 interactions.</p></div>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Composition and state of the inner core?","back":"<b>Solid</b>, mainly <b>iron and nickel</b>."},{"front":"Which layer produces Earth's magnetic field?","back":"The <b>liquid outer core</b>."},{"front":"What drives plate tectonics?","back":"<b>Convection currents</b> in the semi-solid mantle."}],"cardCount":8,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m1-biosphere","title":"Biosphere","short":"Biosphere","blurb":"What the biosphere is, its living components and ecosystems, its functions, and how living things drive the carbon, nitrogen, oxygen and phosphorus cycles.","source":"Lecture W1S3, pages 12–14","tabs":[{"title":"Components and ecosystems","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Biosphere: <b>global sum of all ecosystems</b>, bacteria to whales, on land, water, air.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-biosphere-diagram\"></figure>\n          <div class=\"kv\">\n            <div><span>Plants</span><b>Primary producers</b> (photosynthesis); base of food chains</div>\n            <div><span>Animals</span><b>Consumers</b>: herbivores, carnivores, omnivores</div>\n            <div><span>Microorganisms</span>Bacteria, fungi, algae <b>decompose</b>, recycle nutrients, build soil</div>\n          </div>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-food-chain-cycle\"></figure>\n          <p><b>Ecosystem</b>: <b>biotic</b> community + <b>abiotic</b> parts (sunlight, soil, air, water).</p>\n          <div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Forests</span><span class=\"chip\">Grasslands</span><span class=\"chip\">Deserts</span><span class=\"chip\">Freshwater (rivers, lakes)</span><span class=\"chip\">Marine (oceans, coral reefs)</span></div>\n        "},{"title":"Functions and cycles","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Functions: <b>supports life</b>, <b>maintains biodiversity</b> (more resilience), <b>regulates biogeochemical cycles</b>.</p>\n          <figure data-fig=\"m1-biogeochemical-components\"></figure>\n          <table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>Cycle</th><th>Role of life</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n            <tr><td>Carbon</td><td>Plants fix <b>CO<sub>2</sub></b>; respiration, decay, burning release it</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Nitrogen</td><td>Bacteria fix <b>atmospheric N</b>; <b>denitrification</b> returns it</td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Oxygen</td><td>Plants make O<sub>2</sub>; animals use it, give CO<sub>2</sub></td></tr>\n            <tr><td>Phosphorus</td><td>Taken from soil for <b>DNA, energy transfer</b>; decomposers return it</td></tr>\n          </tbody></table>\n          <div class=\"note exam\"><p>Long answer: definition, producers/consumers/decomposers, ecosystem + 5 examples, 3 functions, 4 cycles.</p></div>\n"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Definition of the biosphere?","back":"The <b>global sum of all ecosystems</b> where life exists on Earth."},{"front":"Role of plants in the biosphere?","back":"<b>Primary producers</b>; base of food chains."},{"front":"Role of microorganisms in the biosphere?","back":"<b>Decompose</b> dead matter, recycle nutrients, add to soil fertility."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0}],"figures":[{"id":"m1-four-spheres","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p11.png","crop":[0.1,0.48,0.52,0.355],"caption":"Earth sits in the middle of the four spheres: atmosphere, hydrosphere, biosphere and lithosphere.","alt":"Circle divided into four photo segments labelled atmosphere, hydrosphere, biosphere and lithosphere around a picture of Earth."},{"id":"m1-air-composition","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p02.png","crop":[0.25,0.14,0.55,0.22],"caption":"Nitrogen is the big slice (78%), oxygen next (21%), and everything else is a sliver (1%).","alt":"Pie chart of air composition with nitrogen, oxygen and others."},{"id":"m1-atmosphere-layers-photo","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p02.png","crop":[0.13,0.53,0.79,0.35],"caption":"The five layers stacked above Earth, troposphere at the bottom and exosphere at the top.","alt":"Photo of Earth from space with bands labelled troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere, thermosphere and exosphere."},{"id":"m1-stratosphere-profile","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p03.png","crop":[0.1,0.64,0.8,0.35],"caption":"Weather and clouds stay below the tropopause; the ozone layer, weather balloons and spy planes sit in the stratosphere; the red line shows temperature turning upward above the tropopause.","alt":"Altitude chart from the troposphere to the mesosphere with a temperature curve, aircraft, balloon, ozone layer and meteors."},{"id":"m1-ozone-uv","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p04.png","crop":[0.1,0.178,0.79,0.322],"caption":"Stratospheric ozone stops most incoming UV; surface-level ozone near cities is smog.","alt":"Diagram of UV-A, UV-B and UV-C rays reaching the stratospheric ozone layer above the troposphere."},{"id":"m1-mesosphere-diagram","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p05.png","crop":[0.1,0.133,0.8,0.347],"caption":"Troposphere, stratosphere with the ozone layer, and mesosphere, separated by the tropopause, stratopause and mesopause.","alt":"Curved bands showing troposphere, stratosphere including ozone layer and mesosphere, with UV and methane molecules."},{"id":"m1-atmosphere-poster","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p06.png","crop":[0.1,0.29,0.79,0.445],"caption":"Each layer with what you find there: planes in the troposphere, the ozone layer in the stratosphere, meteors in the mesosphere, satellites and auroras higher up.","alt":"Poster of the atmosphere layers with heights and objects in each layer."},{"id":"m1-water-cycle-spheres","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p08.png","crop":[0.17,0.18,0.62,0.32],"caption":"Evaporation, condensation, precipitation, runoff and collection make one loop.","alt":"Water cycle illustration with evaporation, condensation, precipitation, runoff and collection labels."},{"id":"m1-earth-layers","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p09.png","crop":[0.1,0.07,0.8,0.36],"caption":"From the outside in: crust, mantle, outer core and inner core.","alt":"Cut-away of Earth showing crust, mantle, outer core and inner core."},{"id":"m1-biosphere-diagram","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p12.png","crop":[0.12,0.34,0.53,0.37],"caption":"The biosphere overlaps the other three spheres: life is found in air, water and earth.","alt":"Illustration of the biosphere with labelled zones for atmosphere, hydrosphere and lithosphere."},{"id":"m1-food-chain-cycle","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p13.png","crop":[0.1,0.13,0.43,0.34],"caption":"Producer to primary, secondary, tertiary and final consumers, with fungi returning matter to the start.","alt":"Circular food chain from green plant through grasshopper, frog, snake and hawk, with fungi."},{"id":"m1-biogeochemical-components","src":"pages/m1/evs-w1-02/p14.png","crop":[0.1,0.178,0.55,0.268],"caption":"The biosphere sits on top of five cycles: carbon, phosphorus, nitrogen, water and oxygen.","alt":"Graphic of the biosphere above five columns labelled carbon, phosphorus, nitrogen, water and oxygen cycle."}]});
